# modules/dast/passive/checks-cors.rules - the §7.1 CORS origin-reflection
# script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md
# DAST-08, tier 2).  Owned by modules/dast/passive/cors.sh; ids are all
# `DAST-CORS-*`.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING PHASE SCRIPT, per rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's
# `checks-<name>.rules` row (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29).  This registry was
# originally meant to be a single co-owned `checks.rules` that every tier-2
# passive ticket appended to - DAST-05, DAST-06, DAST-07, DAST-09, DAST-10 and
# DAST-11 landed under that convention and were later split into their own
# `checks-<name>.rules` files for the reason tension 29 records: peers with no
# ordering between them produced add/add conflicts on the one shared file. This
# ticket (DAST-08) lands after that split, so it is seeded directly under the
# now-current `checks-cors.rules` name rather than the shared file its own
# commit message describes - see modules/dast/passive/checks-headers.rules for
# the full history of the split.  `checks_registry_load` (lib/checks.sh) globs
# every `*.rules` under `modules/dast/` at any depth with no per-file allowlist,
# so this file loads alongside its siblings with no registration step.  It
# mirrors modules/dast/active/checks.rules, which plays the same role for §7.3.
#
# The type tag is `passive`, matching the phase table's `passive/cors.sh:passive`
# floor (modules/dast/engine.sh), so these run at every intensity including the
# default.  They also carry the `quick` profile tag, which
# `docs/DESIGN.md` §5 defines as "passive + config-read, no active probes" -
# that is precisely what a §7.1 check is, and omitting it would leave
# `--profile-scan quick` selecting no DAST check at all.  None is `intrusive`:
# the probe is a single GET/HEAD carrying one extra request header and reads
# only response headers, so there is no state to mutate and no second,
# undocumented opt-in to demand.
#
# `coverage-scope: target` is DAST's cell (the config/scope.conf target id,
# §9.5.1), which is what tension 12 partitions coverage on so a run that visited
# one target cannot manufacture `fixed` findings for another.
#
# THREE CHECK IDS FOR ONE PROBE, ON PURPOSE.  The DAST finding fingerprint's
# location profile is (target, method, path_template, param_location,
# param_name) and carries no verdict component, so a wildcard and a reflection
# observed on the same route under one id would collide and dedupe to a single
# finding.  They are also genuinely different problems with different severity
# and different remediation - see modules/dast/passive/cors_engine.sh's
# `cors_classify` for the full argument - which is the same reasoning
# modules/dast/active/checks.rules records for its own three SQLi ids.
#
# TWO MORE IDS ADDED BY THE DAST-08 FOLLOW-UP ("detect Access-Control-Allow-
# Origin reflecting the null origin"): DAST-CORS-NULL_ORIGIN-01 and
# DAST-CORS-NULL_ORIGIN_WITH_CREDENTIALS-01, from a SECOND, `Origin: null`
# probe modules/dast/passive/cors.sh sends only when the sentinel probe's own
# verdict was neither `reflected` nor `wildcard`.  The plain/credentialed
# split mirrors DAST-CORS-ORIGIN_REFLECTED-01/-REFLECTED_WITH_CREDENTIALS-01
# for the identical reason: severity is a fixed per-check-id registry field,
# so a verdict whose severity depends on Access-Control-Allow-Credentials
# cannot be one id with a severity raised at runtime - see
# modules/dast/passive/cors_engine.sh's `cors_null_reflected`.

id: DAST-CORS-NULL_ORIGIN-01
title: CORS null-origin trust (arbitrary attacker-controlled context accepted)
script: passive/cors.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-346
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Do not treat the literal null origin as trusted in
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin. A browser sends Origin: null for a sandboxed
  iframe, a data: URL document, and several redirect shapes - contexts an
  attacker fully controls - so accepting it is equivalent to accepting any
  origin whatsoever. Compare the request Origin against a server-side
  allowlist of exact origins and emit no CORS header at all (or the stored
  allowlisted value) when the Origin is null or otherwise unrecognised; never
  special-case null as an allowed value. Add Vary: Origin so a shared cache
  cannot serve one origin the response minted for another.
references: CWE-346
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
references: https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#http-cors-protocol
references: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Same-origin_policy

id: DAST-CORS-NULL_ORIGIN_WITH_CREDENTIALS-01
title: CORS null-origin trust with credentials allowed
script: passive/cors.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-346
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: This route trusts the literal null origin AND allows
  credentials, so any attacker-controlled context that sends Origin: null - a
  sandboxed iframe, a data: URL document, several redirect shapes - can read
  this response with the victim's cookies attached. Replace the null
  special-case with a server-side allowlist of exact origins and emit the
  stored value, never a default that admits null; keep
  Access-Control-Allow-Credentials off for every origin that is not on that
  allowlist. Add Vary: Origin so a shared cache cannot serve one origin the
  response minted for another.
references: CWE-346
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
references: https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#http-cors-protocol
references: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Same-origin_policy

id: DAST-CORS-ORIGIN_REFLECTED-01
title: CORS origin reflection (arbitrary origin trusted)
script: passive/cors.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-346
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Do not echo the request Origin into Access-Control-Allow-Origin.
  Compare the Origin against a server-side allowlist of exact origins (scheme,
  host and port) and emit that stored value, or emit no CORS header at all when
  it does not match; never derive the header from the request. Reject on
  mismatch rather than falling back to a permissive default, and add
  Vary: Origin so a shared cache cannot serve one origin the response minted
  for another. Check any framework CORS middleware for a wildcard, regex or
  suffix match that accepts more origins than intended.
references: CWE-346
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
references: https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#http-cors-protocol

id: DAST-CORS-REFLECTED_WITH_CREDENTIALS-01
title: CORS origin reflection with credentials allowed
script: passive/cors.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-346
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: This route echoes the request Origin AND allows credentials, so
  any web page on any origin can read this response with the victim's cookies
  attached. Replace the echo with a server-side allowlist of exact origins and
  emit the stored value, never the request's own; keep
  Access-Control-Allow-Credentials off for every origin that is not on that
  allowlist. Add Vary: Origin so a shared cache cannot serve one origin the
  response minted for another, and treat any framework CORS middleware
  configured with a wildcard, regex or suffix origin match as the same bug.
references: CWE-346
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
references: https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#http-cors-protocol

id: DAST-CORS-WILDCARD-01
title: CORS wildcard: Access-Control-Allow-Origin is *
script: passive/cors.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-942
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Access-Control-Allow-Origin with a literal asterisk makes this
  response readable by script from every origin on the internet. That is
  correct for a genuinely public, unauthenticated asset and wrong for anything
  else: replace it with a server-side allowlist of the exact origins that need
  cross-origin access, and confirm the resource carries no user-specific or
  otherwise non-public data. A browser refuses to combine a wildcard with
  credentials, so this is not itself a route to authenticated data - but do not
  "fix" that by echoing the request Origin instead, which is a strictly worse
  origin-reflection vulnerability.
references: CWE-942
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
references: https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#http-cors-protocol
